course: everyday
lesson: 369
title: Getting help is not telling tales
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: For three weeks Sam walks the long way round to school to avoid the boys at the bus shelter. He's late twice, his shoes wear thin and he tells nobody. Then one evening he tells his mum everything. She doesn't laugh. She rings the school, and the next week a teacher stands at that corner at half past eight. Sam walks the short way again — and wonders why he waited three weeks.
explain: Lily is worried about «telling tales», because that is what the boys sneer at. So she asks her teacher what the difference is. The teacher says: «Telling tales is trying to get someone into trouble over something small — like who pushed in the lunch queue. Getting help is when someone is hurt, in danger, or being picked on again and again. Getting help is always right.»
explain: Not everyone gets it right first time. Maddie twists her ankle in a scrap behind the garages and tells no one, so it swells to twice its size; the doctor says she should have had ice and a check-up the same day. Jake's dad tells him «never grass on anyone». But when older boys from the secondary school push Jake around, for once Jake tells a teacher instead of fighting — and, to his amazement, it works.
explain: Sam keeps learning. When a big Station Road boy knocks a little girl off her scooter, Sam runs to the shop on the corner and tells the man at the counter, who comes straight out. When he finds Lily crying behind the mobile classroom because a Station Road girl took her phone, he doesn't try to grab it back — he fetches the teacher on duty. And after weeks of being called names, he rings Childline from his gran's phone. A kind voice listens and helps him work out what to do next.
fact: Telling tales means trying to get someone into trouble over something small; getting help means someone is hurt, in danger or being bullied.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about bullying or danger is always the right thing, even if others call it grassing or snitching.
fact: If a child is hurt in the street, a nearby adult such as a shopkeeper can help quickly; in an emergency adults call 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA and Canada, or 000 in Australia.
fact: Childline (0800 1111 in the UK) is a free helpline where children can talk about any worry; other countries have their own, like Kids Help Phone in Canada and Kids Helpline in Australia.
fact: A twisted ankle should be rested, cooled with ice wrapped in a cloth, and checked by an adult or doctor if it swells or hurts a lot.
fact: Hiding an injury so you don't get in trouble can make it much worse.
fact: Trying to grab back something stolen can turn into a fight; fetching an adult is safer.
fact: Older children picking on younger ones is something adults must know about; it is not for the younger child to fix alone.
fact: Problems kept secret for a long time usually get bigger, not smaller.
fact: Grown-ups can be wrong about «never telling»; keeping children safe matters more than any playground rule.
exercise: Why did Sam walk the long way round for three weeks? | He was scared of the boys at the bus shelter | He liked the view of the fields on the long way round | His mum told him to | The short way was closed
exercise: What happened once Sam told his mum? | The school put a teacher at the corner | His mum told him to toughen up and walk the short way | Nothing changed at all | The bus shelter was knocked down
exercise: What is the difference between telling tales and getting help? | Getting help is about harm or danger; tales are small things | There is no difference at all, both are just snitching | Telling tales is always about danger | Getting help means you are a snitch
exercise: Which of these is getting help, not telling tales? | Telling a teacher someone is hit every day | Telling a teacher someone pushed in the queue | Telling a teacher someone has messy writing | Telling a teacher who sneezed loudly in assembly
exercise: Why did Maddie's ankle swell to twice its size? | She hid the injury and didn't get it treated | She played football on it for fun | The ice she put on it made the swelling much worse | The doctor bandaged it too tight
exercise: What should Maddie have done when she twisted her ankle? | Tell an adult, rest it and cool it with wrapped ice | Run it off and forget about it | Hide it until it stopped hurting | Put a hot water bottle straight on it and keep walking
exercise: Jake's dad says «never grass on anyone». What is true? | Telling an adult about bullying is right, whatever anyone says | Grown-ups like Jake's dad are never wrong about this sort of thing | Jake should always fight instead | Only girls are allowed to tell
exercise: Why was it wise for Jake to tell a teacher about the secondary school boys? | They were older and bigger; adults can stop them safely | Because Jake wanted them expelled just for fun | Because he was too lazy to fight | Because the teacher was his uncle
exercise: Why did Sam run to the shopkeeper when the girl fell off her scooter? | A nearby adult could help her quickly | To buy her some sweets | Because Sam wanted to fight the big boy | To hide from the Station Road boy
exercise: Why didn't Sam grab Lily's phone back himself? | Grabbing it could start a fight; the teacher could sort it | Because he didn't like Lily's phone very much | Because he was too small to reach it | Because the phone was broken anyway
exercise: What is Childline? | A free helpline where children can talk about worries | A shop in town that sells cheap phones to children | A club for children who fight | A game played on the telephone
exercise: How did Sam probably feel after the call to Childline? | Listened to and less alone | Ashamed and in even more trouble | Angry at the person on the phone | Exactly the same as before the call
exercise: What lesson did Sam learn from waiting three weeks? | Telling sooner would have saved him weeks of worry | He should have waited even longer, until the end of term | Mums never help with school problems | Long walks always fix bullying
exercise: Lily asked her teacher about telling tales. Why was that brave? | She asked even though the boys sneer at telling | Because her teacher is very scary | Because she wanted to get the boys expelled from school | Because she already knew the answer
exercise: Which of these is a bad reason to keep a problem secret? | Being scared someone will call you a grass | The problem is already completely solved | It was only a tiny thing that didn't matter | You told an adult who's sorting it
exercise: What usually happens to problems kept secret for a long time? | They get bigger, not smaller | They always disappear on their own | They turn into good luck later | They become someone else's problem
test: «u told ur MUM?? grass». Sam's been dodging the bus shelter boys for 3 weeks. Was he right? | Yes, it was getting help, not grassing | No, he should have sorted it all out by himself | No, he should've waited longer | Yes, but only because he's a baby
test: «Miss, Freddie took 2 grapes not 1!!» Tales or getting help? | Telling tales, it's a tiny thing | Getting help, it's a real emergency | Getting help, because grapes matter most | Neither, because it's a serious crime
test: «Miss, a Year 6 keeps punching Ollie at break». Tales or getting help? | Getting help, someone's being hurt | Telling tales, it's not your business | Getting help, but only if Ollie asks | Neither, punches don't count at break
test: Maddie, limping: «nah don't tell anyone, I'll be fine». Her ankle's going purple. What should a friend do? | Get an adult now, even if she's cross | Leave it, she said she's fine and to go away | Tell her to run on it so it loosens up | Push hard on it to see if it's really broken
test: Jake's dad: «A real man never grasses». Jake's getting shoved about by older lads. Best choice for Jake? | Tell a teacher and let adults sort it | Fight all of them by himself after school | Stay home from school forever | Bring a weapon to scare them
test: little girl's knocked off her scooter, crying, big lad legging it. ur 9. what's the move | Get the nearest adult, like the shopkeeper | Chase the big lad down the street and tackle him | Walk on, she'll be OK | Film it on your phone
test: «she nicked my phone» — Lily's sobbing. «I'll get it back, I'll smack her», says Kieran. Wiser idea? | Fetch the teacher on duty right now | Let Kieran go and smack her to get it back | Grab it back yourself | Nick her phone back in return
test: «nobody at home listens, the name-calling's been weeks». What's a good thing to try? | Ring a helpline like Childline or tell another trusted adult | Give up and say nothing to anyone, ever | Call them worse names every day | Wait till it gets worse
test: «telling is for babies, mate». Which is true? | Getting help when someone's hurt is right at any age | Only babies need help; big kids sort it out alone | Grown-ups never need help | Telling makes everything worse every time
test: Teacher: «Why didn't you tell me sooner, Sam?» What's the most honest likely reason? | He was scared of being called a grass | He forgot for three weeks | He wanted the long walk home every day | He thought the teacher was busy
test: «I'm not grassing, I'll get him back myself after school». What's likely to happen? | A fight where someone gets hurt and both get in trouble | Everything sorted out forever, with no trouble at all | The other boy becomes his friend | A medal from the head teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately distinguishes «telling tales» from «seeking assistance»? | Seeking assistance concerns harm, danger or repeated bullying | Seeking assistance concerns trivial matters only | The two terms are entirely interchangeable in ordinary usage | Telling tales concerns injuries requiring a doctor
exam: Given that a pupil has sustained a twisted ankle and concealed the injury, identify the principal consequence observed. | The swelling increased substantially for want of treatment | The injury resolved more rapidly without intervention of any kind | The concealment prevented any further harm | The ankle was strengthened by continued use
exam: Identify the most appropriate course of action for a pupil who discovers that a classmate's telephone has been taken by another child. | To summon the teacher on duty without delay | To retrieve the telephone by force | To take an article belonging to the other child | To refrain from any action whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a parental instruction never to inform on others. | The safety of children takes precedence over such an instruction | Such an instruction must be obeyed in every circumstance | Such an instruction applies to injuries, but not to cases of bullying | Such an instruction is endorsed by all schools
